\mt1 Readings of the Byzantine Text
\ip The New Testament of this edition is the King James Version conformed to the Byzantine text of the Greek New Testament as edited by Maurice A. Robinson and William G. Pierpont (2026). The King James translators followed the Received Text, the Greek printed by Erasmus, Stephanus and Beza, as F. H. A. Scrivener reconstructed it from their choices in 1894. The two Greek texts differ in some nineteen hundred places. Where the difference can be seen in English, the words of the King James Version are changed, as little as the Greek requires and in its own English, and a note marked TR gives the words it had; where the difference cannot be seen in English, nothing is changed. Four verses of the Received Text that the Byzantine manuscripts lack are omitted, each quoted in a note on the verse before it; the doxology of Romans stands at {ROM 14:24-26}, where those manuscripts have it; and {MAT 23:13} and {MAT 23:14} exchange places.\f + \ft The English changes are based on William G. Pierpont’s Some Improvements to the King James Version from the Majority Greek Manuscripts (1990). Where an instruction cannot be applied or its wording does not fit, a fitting parallel in the King James Version is preferred, then Pierpont’s wording, the Revised Version of 1881, and Robert Adam Boyd’s American Standard Version, Byzantine Text (2021). Boyd’s Text-Critical English New Testament (2022; © 2021 Robert Adam Boyd; CC BY 4.0, https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/; source: https://archive.org/details/TCENT) and Graham G. Thomason’s Far Above All are occasionally consulted, in that order. Borrowed wording is adapted into the English of the King James Version. Robinson’s Greek texts and his collation of Scrivener with them, Boyd’s Text-Critical Greek New Testament, the Majority Standard Bible, the World English Bible, the American Standard Version of 1901, and the CrossWire Bible Society’s tagging of the King James Version were also consulted.\f*
\ip This appendix lists every verse in which the two Greek texts differ, book by book, under the number of this edition; a verse that the King James Bible numbers otherwise gives that number too. Where the English changes, the entry gives the Greek of both texts and then the English of both Bibles, each on its own line: TR for the Received Text, RP for the Byzantine text, KJV for the King James Version and OLEB for this edition. Where the English is the same in both, the entry gives only the Greek words that differ. The Greek is printed with accents and breathings; the verses the Byzantine text lacks are listed where the King James Bible has them.
{readings}
